3:8: warning: A bare glob can expand to a filename that begins with '-'.
     3 |    grep *.txt file
       |         ^~~~~
note: Prefix the glob with a directory or place -- before it.
3:8: warning: The unquoted grep pattern can glob against the local files before grep sees it.
     3 |    grep *.txt file
       |         ^~~~~
note: Quote the pattern.
4:8: warning: A grep reads a regular expression, where a leading * has nothing to repeat, this pattern looks like a glob.
     4 |    grep '*x' file
       |         ^~~~
5:12: warning: A for over the cat output iterates IFS-split words rather than lines.
     5 |    for f in $(cat list); do echo "$f"; done
       |             ^~~~~~~~~~~
note: Read the lines with 'while IFS= read -r line' instead.
6:12: warning: A for over the find output breaks a name with whitespace apart.
     6 |    for f in $(find . -name x); do echo "$f"; done
       |             ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Use find -exec or a 'while read -r' loop over find -print0.
7:12: warning: An xargs splits the find output on whitespace and quotes.
     7 |    find . | xargs echo
       |             ^~~~~
note: Pair find -print0 with xargs -0 or use find -exec.
8:10: warning: The pipe feeds 'echo', which never reads stdin, so the upstream output is discarded.
     8 |    true | echo discarded
       |           ^~~~
13:18: warning: 2>&1 before the file redirect duplicates the terminal, so stderr stays on the terminal.
    13 |    command 2>&1 > out.log
       |                   ^~~~~~~
note: Put the file redirect first as in '>file 2>&1'.
14:17: warning: The command reads and truncates 'data' at once, the truncation empties the input before it is read.
    14 |    sort < data > data
       |                  ^~~~
14:10: details:
    14 |    sort < data > data
       |           ^~~~ this redirect reads the file that is later truncated.
note: Write to a temporary and move it over.
15:8: warning: A command substitution wraps a useless echo.
    15 |    echo "wrapped $(echo inner)"
       |         ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: The text can be used directly without the subshell.
16:15: warning: $@ inside a longer word concatenates the surrounding text onto the first and last argument.
    16 |    printf '%s' "a $@ b"
       |                ^~~~~~~~
note: Use $* for one joined string or a separate "$@" word.
21:8: warning: The operand is a literal, so this -z test is constant.
    21 |    [ -z lit ] && echo a
       |         ^~~
note: Test a variable or drop the check.
22:5: warning: The numeric comparison -eq reads 'count', which is not a number, so the test errors at run time.
    22 |    [ count -eq notanumber ] && echo b
       |      ^~~~~
22:15: warning: The numeric comparison -eq reads 'notanumber', which is not a number, so the test errors at run time.
    22 |    [ count -eq notanumber ] && echo b
       |                ^~~~~~~~~~
23:12: warning: [ and test compare strings byte for byte and never glob-match.
    23 |    [ "$x" = "glob*" ] && echo c
       |             ^~~~~~~
note: Use a case or the [[ ]] form for the pattern.
28:6: warning: This case has no default *) branch, a value no pattern matches is silently ignored.
    28 |  case a in a) ;; esac
       |       ^
34:3: warning: The egrep command is deprecated.
    34 |    egrep pattern file
       |    ^~~~~
note: Use grep -E for the extended regular expression match.
35:3: warning: The fgrep command is deprecated.
    35 |    fgrep word file
       |    ^~~~~
note: Use grep -F for the fixed string match.
36:3: warning: An expr forks for arithmetic the shell does natively.
    36 |    expr 1 + 2
       |    ^~~~
note: Use $((...)) for the calculation.
38:3: warning: An echo of a command substitution prints what the command already prints.
    38 |    echo "$(date)"
       |    ^~~~
note: Run the command on its own instead.
39:8: warning: Grepping the ps output races the process table and matches the grep itself.
    39 |    ps | grep init
       |         ^~~~
note: Use pgrep to match a process by name.
40:8: warning: Grepping the ls listing mangles a name with a space or a newline.
    40 |    ls | grep conf
       |         ^~~~
note: Match the names with a glob or with find instead.
41:3: warning: Counting grep output with wc -l runs an extra process.
    41 |    grep needle file | wc -l
       |    ^~~~
note: Use grep -c to count the matching lines directly.
43:5: warning: A test reads an unquoted variable.
    43 |    [ $? -eq 0 ] && echo ok
       |      ^~
note: Quote it to avoid an empty or split argument.
43:5: warning: Testing $? checks the exit status indirectly.
    43 |    [ $? -eq 0 ] && echo ok
       |      ^~
note: Test the command directly with if or && so an intervening command cannot clobber the status.
44:9: warning: Declaring and assigning from a command substitution in one command masks the command's exit status.
    44 |    local declared=$(date)
       |          ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Split the declaration and the assignment so a failure is seen.
48:7: warning: This assignment to 'x' in a function has no local, so the value leaks to the global scope.
    48 |  f() { x=1; }
       |        ^~~
note: Declare it with local to keep it inside the function.
50:7: warning: This assignment to 'z' in a function has no local, so the value leaks to the global scope.
    50 |  h() { z=4; }
       |        ^~~
note: Declare it with local to keep it inside the function.
58:3: warning: A negated -eq is just -ne.
    58 |  [ ! 1 -eq 2 ]
       |    ^
note: Drop the ! and use -ne.
59:3: warning: A negated = is just !=.
    59 |  [ ! a = b ]
       |    ^
note: Drop the ! and use !=.
62:8: warning: The printf format comes from a variable, the data can inject format directives.
    62 |  printf "$1"
       |         ^~~~
note: Use printf '%s' to print it.
64:11: warning: The printf format comes from a variable, the data can inject format directives.
    64 |  printf -- "$1"
       |            ^~~~
note: Use printf '%s' to print it.
66:1: warning: A read without -r mangles a backslash in the input.
    66 |  read x < /dev/null
       |  ^~~~~~
note: Add -r to read the line literally.
69:8: warning: A test with -a or -o is obsolescent.
    69 |  [ -f a -a -f b ]
       |         ^~
note: Join two tests with && or || instead.
70:3: warning: A negated -z is just -n.
    70 |  [ ! -z "$x" ]
       |    ^
note: Test with -n instead.
71:3: warning: A negated -n is just -z.
    71 |  [ ! -n "$y" ]
       |    ^
note: Test with -z instead.
74:1: warning: An echo of a command substitution prints what the command already prints.
    74 |  echo $(echo hi)
       |  ^~~~
note: Run the command on its own instead.
74:6: warning: An unquoted command substitution splits its output.
    74 |  echo $(echo hi)
       |       ^~~~~~~~~~
note: Quote it to keep one argument.
74:6: warning: A command substitution wraps a useless echo.
    74 |  echo $(echo hi)
       |       ^~~~~~~~~~
note: The text can be used directly without the subshell.
75:8: warning: Declaring and assigning from a command substitution in one command masks the command's exit status.
    75 |  export FOO=$(echo hi)
       |         ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Split the declaration and the assignment so a failure is seen.
76:1: warning: An echo of a command substitution prints what the command already prints.
    76 |  echo "$(echo hi)"
       |  ^~~~
note: Run the command on its own instead.
76:6: warning: A command substitution wraps a useless echo.
    76 |  echo "$(echo hi)"
       |       ^~~~~~~~~~~~
note: The text can be used directly without the subshell.
78:1: warning: A useless cat.
    78 |  cat data/hello-d | cat
       |  ^~~
note: Give the file to the next command directly instead of piping cat.
94:3: warning: Commands after exec do not run when exec succeeds.
    94 |    exec echo replaced; echo unreachable
       |    ^~~~
94:23: details:
    94 |    exec echo replaced; echo unreachable
       |                        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ this is the first command skipped after exec.
note: Remove exec or remove the unreachable commands.
95:3: warning: This cd is unchecked, so later commands can run in the wrong directory.
    95 |    cd missing; echo wrong-directory
       |    ^~
note: Stop or return when cd fails.
96:17: warning: A && B || C also runs C when B fails.
    96 |    true && false || echo fallback
       |                  ^~
note: Use an if statement when C is the else branch.
83:15: warning: A for loop over ls output breaks filenames at whitespace.
    83 |    for file in $(ls *.mp3); do echo "$file"; done
       |                ^~~~~~~~~~~
note: Iterate over a glob or read a delimited filename stream.
84:15: warning: A quoted for-loop glob remains one literal word.
    84 |    for file in "*.mp3"; do echo "$file"; done
       |                ^~~~~~~
note: Leave the glob unquoted so it expands into loop values.
86:16: warning: The unquoted find pattern expands before find sees it.
    86 |    find . -name *.ogg
       |                 ^~~~~
note: Quote the pattern so find performs the match.
86:16: warning: A bare glob can expand to a filename that begins with '-'.
    86 |    find . -name *.ogg
       |                 ^~~~~
note: Prefix the glob with a directory or place -- before it.
87:22: warning: The find result is inserted into shell source and can execute filename text.
    87 |    find . -exec sh -c 'echo {}' \;
       |                       ^~~~~~~~~
note: Pass the result as an argument and reference it as $1.
88:9: warning: An unquoted unset array index can expand as a filename glob.
    88 |    unset values[0]
       |          ^~~~~~~~~
note: Quote the complete array element name.
89:8: warning: A read operand is a variable name, not a variable value.
    89 |    read $target
       |         ^~~~~~~
note: Drop the dollar sign from the variable name.
89:3: warning: A read without -r mangles a backslash in the input.
    89 |    read $target
       |    ^~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Add -r to read the line literally.
92:10: warning: The printf format consumes more arguments than the command supplies.
    92 |    printf '%s %s\n' one
       |           ^~~~~~~~~
note: Add the missing arguments or remove format directives.
93:60: warning: The shell opens this redirection before sudo changes privileges.
    93 |    command -v sudo >/dev/null 2>&1 && sudo echo protected > output
       |                                                             ^~~~~~
note: Run a shell under sudo or pipe through sudo tee.
97:22: warning: This pipeline read assignment is lost when the stage exits.
    97 |    echo one | read -r pipeline_value
       |                       ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Feed the loop with a redirection or process substitution.
98:8: warning: This read sees the value from before the pipeline.
    98 |    echo "$pipeline_value"
       |         ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Move the assignment outside the pipeline or avoid the pipeline subshell.
99:3: warning: The > operator compares strings lexicographically.
    99 |    [[ 10 > 7 ]]
       |    ^~
note: Use an arithmetic command or a numeric -lt or -gt operator.
99:3: warning: The '>' comparison has literal operands on both sides.
    99 |    [[ 10 > 7 ]]
       |    ^~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Remove the constant condition or compare runtime data.
100:3: warning: The '<' comparison has literal operands on both sides.
   100 |    [[ left < right ]]
       |    ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Remove the constant condition or compare runtime data.
101:15: warning: A quoted regular expression is matched literally.
   101 |    [[ value =~ 'v.*' ]]
       |                ^~~~~
note: Store the expression in a variable or leave it unquoted.
102:9: warning: A file test on a glob checks only one expanded path.
   102 |    [[ -e *.txt ]]
       |          ^~~~~
note: Expand the glob in a loop and test each path.
103:6: warning: A conditional operator needs surrounding spaces.
   103 |    [[ $left=$right ]]
       |       ^~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Place spaces around the comparison operator.
104:9: warning: The operand is literal, so this string test is constant.
   104 |    [[ -n literal ]]
       |          ^~~~~~~
note: Test a variable or drop the check.
105:8: warning: Brace ranges are expanded before variables.
   105 |    echo {1..$limit}
       |         ^~~~~~~~~~~
note: Use an arithmetic loop for a variable limit.
105:8: warning: An unquoted variable can split into words and expand globs.
   105 |    echo {1..$limit}
       |         ^~~~~~~~~~~
note: Quote the expansion to keep one argument.
106:6: warning: Brackets around a tr range add literal bracket bytes.
   106 |    tr [A-Z] [a-z]
       |       ^~~~~
note: Use a quoted range without brackets.
106:12: warning: Brackets around a tr range add literal bracket bytes.
   106 |    tr [A-Z] [a-z]
       |             ^~~~~
note: Use a quoted range without brackets.
106:6: warning: The unquoted tr range can expand as a filename glob.
   106 |    tr [A-Z] [a-z]
       |       ^~~~~
note: Quote the range.
106:12: warning: The unquoted tr range can expand as a filename glob.
   106 |    tr [A-Z] [a-z]
       |             ^~~~~
note: Quote the range.
107:3: warning: This assignment to 'index' in a function has no local, so the value leaks to the global scope.
   107 |    index=0
       |    ^~~~~~~
note: Declare it with local to keep it inside the function.
108:21: warning: A redirection expansion can run in a child and lose its mutation.
   108 |    echo redirected > "file$((index++))"
       |                      ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
note: Update the variable before forming the redirect path.
109:8: warning: An unquoted variable can split into words and expand globs.
   109 |    echo $value
       |         ^~~~~~
note: Quote the expansion to keep one argument.
110:8: warning: A quoted tilde stays literal instead of expanding to the home directory.
   110 |    echo "~"
       |         ^~~
note: Leave the tilde unquoted or use a quoted $HOME expansion.
111:8: warning: Single quotes prevent the expansion written inside them.
   111 |    echo '$value'
       |         ^~~~~~~~
note: Use double quotes if the value should expand.
113:8: warning: External input is evaluated as arithmetic code.
   113 |    echo $((number + 1))
